</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">To manage the complexity of transformers in video compression, local attention mechanisms are a practical necessity. The common approach of partitioning frames into patches, however, creates architectural flaws like irregular receptive fields. When adapted for temporal autoregressive models, this paradigm, exemplified by the <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">Video Compression Transformer (VCT)</span>, also necessitates computationally redundant overlapping windows. This work introduces 3D <span title="Sliding Window Attention" class="ltx_glossaryref">Sliding Window Attention (SWA)</span>, a patchless form of local attention. By enabling a decoder-only architecture that unifies spatial and temporal context processing, and by providing a uniform receptive field, our method significantly improves rate-distortion performance, achieving Bjøntegaard Delta-rate savings of up to <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> against the <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span> baseline. Simultaneously, by eliminating the need for overlapping windows, our method reduces overall decoder complexity by a factor of <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math>, while its entropy model is nearly <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5</annotation></semantics></math> times more efficient. We further analyze our model’s behavior and show that while it benefits from long-range temporal context, excessive context can degrade performance.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>learned video compression, transformers, sliding window attention, autoregressive models, entropy coding.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learned video compression is an active research field, with recent <span title="Neural Video Codec" class="ltx_glossaryref">Neural Video Codecs (NVCs)</span> beginning to outperform traditional codecs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. While transformers have become dominant in many computer vision domains, most state-of-the-art <span title="Neural Video Codec" class="ltx_glossaryref">NVCs</span> still rely on <span title="Convolutional Neural Network" class="ltx_glossaryref">Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)</span>. A common strategy to adapt transformers for video is to partition frames into non-overlapping patches, a paradigm used by <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">Video Compression Transformer (VCT)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and Swin-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. However, this approach introduces a key architectural flaw: the receptive field for each hyperpixel is defined relative to its enclosing patch instead of the hyperpixel itself, making the local context position-dependent. This issue is particularly detrimental in a strictly autoregressive setting, where causal masking results in an unequal number of visible neighbors for different hyperpixels within the same patch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The patch-based approach in our primary baseline, <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span>, also introduces hard boundaries that impede information flow between patches. This issue becomes particularly significant for temporal context, as motion can cause features corresponding to a patch in the current frame to lie in entirely different patches in previous frames. To counteract this, <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span> processes larger, overlapping windows for temporal context extraction. This strategy, however, creates significant computational overhead by repeatedly processing the same hyperpixels. Furthermore, its bi-directional temporal encoder is not autoregressive, preventing an efficient causal decoding process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose a decoder-only, fully autoregressive entropy model that uses 3D <span title="Sliding Window Attention" class="ltx_glossaryref">Sliding Window Attention (SWA)</span> to jointly process spatial and temporal context. This unified architecture eliminates artificial boundaries and avoids the redundant computations of <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span>’s overlapping window approach, while providing a uniform receptive field. The proposed model yields <span title="Bjøntegaard Delta" class="ltx_glossaryref">Bjøntegaard Delta (BD)</span>-rate savings of up to <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> over the <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span> baseline, while reducing total decoder kMACs by a factor of <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math>, with the entropy model becoming nearly <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5</annotation></semantics></math> times more efficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <span title="Convolutional Neural Network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> have been standard for learned video compression, recent work increasingly integrates transformers to overcome the limitations of static filters. Transformers are used, for example, to replace the main feature transform with a Swin-based architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, or in hybrid models that run Swin blocks alongside convolutions for both the feature transform and the entropy model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Beyond the feature transform, transformers are also leveraged for other codec components, such as motion compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> or as entropy models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The transformer-based entropy model in our primary baseline, <span title="Video Compression Transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">VCT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, uses a non-autoregressive, bi-directional temporal encoder on overlapping patches from two reference frames, and an autoregressive decoder that fuses this context with spatial information from the current frame’s non-overlapping patches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our work moves away from patching entirely, instead building on local or sliding window attention. This mechanism has been successfully applied to 2D vision tasks as Neighborhood Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. The work of Chen <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> also uses a non-patch-based transformer, but its application within the codec is fundamentally different from ours. They employ channel-wise attention as part of the feature transform and context fusion. In contrast, our model uses spatio-temporal attention exclusively within the entropy model to autoregressively predict the probability distribution of the quantized latents. This paper introduces the first fully spatio-temporal, non-patch-based, and fully autoregressive transformer as an entropy model for learned video compression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Method</span></h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of the proposed method. (a) The 3D <span title="Sliding Window Attention" class="ltx_glossaryref">SWA</span> kernel. For the current hyperpixel (purple), attention is computed over its previously decoded spatio-temporal neighbors (blue). Future hyperpixels that fall within the window (transparent) are causally masked. (b) The proposed transformer block architecture.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sliding Window Attention</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed attention mechanism operates on the latent volume <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H\times W\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H\times W\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are the number of frames, height, width, and channels, respectively. The mechanism is analogous to a 3D convolution, applying a local kernel to each hyperpixel to gather context, as visualized in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ III Proposed Method ‣ Sliding Window Attention for Learned Video Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>. For a given hyperpixel, attention is computed over its spatio-temporal neighbors within the window. Causal masking is applied to ensure that only previously decoded hyperpixels are used, while future ones are ignored. Compared to a standard convolution, our attention kernel has two key differences. First, the weights are not static but are dynamically computed based on the attention scores. Second, no zero-padding is applied at the boundaries of the latent volume, as this would be ill-suited for attention mechanisms due to introducing uninformative tokens into the context. Instead, the attention window is simply truncated, meaning the receptive field becomes progressively smaller for hyperpixels closer to an edge. However, since this effect is confined to the perimeter of the latent volume, its overall impact is minimal.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Autoregressive prediction scheme for a single frame. Decoding follows a line-scan order (green). At the start of each new row, the hyperpixel directly above it is prepended to the input sequence (red), ensuring the local attention window has access to spatially relevant context.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, after flattening <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> into a sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathbb{R}^{(L\cdot H\cdot W)\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathbb{R}^{(L\cdot H\cdot W)\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we follow the standard multi-head attention paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. First, for each of the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads, query (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), key (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and value (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) tensors are computed via linear projections with learnable weight matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}^{Q},W_{i}^{K},W_{i}^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}^{Q},W_{i}^{K},W_{i}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=YW_{i}^{Q},\quad K_{i}=YW_{i}^{K},\quad V_{i}=YW_{i}^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=YW_{i}^{Q},\quad K_{i}=YW_{i}^{K},\quad V_{i}=YW_{i}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The attention output for a single head is then calculated as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{i}K_{i}^{T}+B_{i}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{i}K_{i}^{T}+B_{i}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the scores are scaled by the square root of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension of the query and key vectors. Finally, the outputs of all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads are concatenated and projected with a final weight matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{O}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Concat}(A_{1},\dots,A_{h})W^{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Concat}(A_{1},\dots,A_{h})W^{O}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Our key modification lies in the bias matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{(LHW)\times(LHW)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{(LHW)\times(LHW)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which enforces the local window. Its elements <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{m,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{m,n}</annotation></semantics></math> between hyperpixels <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are defined by their relative positions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{i}^{m,n}=\begin{cases}s_{i}^{\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x}&amp;\text{if }|\Delta l|\leq L_{w},|\Delta y|\leq H_{w},|\Delta x|\leq W_{w}\\
-\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{i}^{m,n}=\begin{cases}s_{i}^{\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x}&amp;\text{if }|\Delta l|\leq L_{w},|\Delta y|\leq H_{w},|\Delta x|\leq W_{w}\\
-\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable tensor of size <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2L_{w}+1)\times(2H_{w}+1)\times(2W_{w}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2L_{w}+1)\times(2H_{w}+1)\times(2W_{w}+1)</annotation></semantics></math> containing the relative positional biases <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{\Delta l,\Delta y,\Delta x}</annotation></semantics></math> for one head, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{w},H_{w},W_{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{w},H_{w},W_{w})</annotation></semantics></math> defines the maximum offset from the center of the window along each axis. For efficient implementation, we adapt the FlashAttention-2 algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> in Triton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> to avoid computing attention for fully masked blocks and to directly incorporate the biases from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Entropy Model</span></h3>

